Giancarlo Pfeifer (pfeifer@griffo.mat.unimi.it) wrote:
: All happened when I decided to kill a process using a "kill -9 #pid" 
: command. The process immediately entered the state of <defunct> process,
: generating at the same time, a comsat process with the status of
: uninterruptable process. Every time I get new mail, the system
: automatically generates a new comsat process.

The comsat processes probably indicate that the system is confused
about whether pfeifer is connected or not.  I've had a similar problem
with users not being on, but being reported as connected by "who" and
the like.  The result is that biff, which is what comsat really is,
get's fired up but doesn't have anywhere to send the notice.  I'm
currently talking to DEC support about this.

I believe the defunct status indicates that the process was killed while
running some kernel code.  The result is that the system can't
completely kill the process, but there's nothing really happening
either.  I just ignore it until the next reboot.

