In article <54rfks$581@nntp.ucs.ubc.ca>, advax@triumf.ca wrote:

>>>
>>>So Digital have in their wisdom imposed some arbitrary small limit
>>>on record lengths in a dbm database.
>>>

   Don't blame Digital.  The header file you showed (minus the copyright
information (tsk,tsk..) is right out of BSD 4.3 (or maybe 4.2...)
with only minor modifications for name-space pollution by OSF.

   NDBM is not supposed to be a general-purpose database, it was intended
for fairly limited things....

   The new XPG4.2 standards raise the size of keys and data,  but they're
still not exactly enormous.  I think the requirement went up to maybe
4kb for the key+data...

   I implemented a rather clever hack that allowed backwards compatible
access to existing ndbm files while allowing new files to dynamically
grow to the larger size.  It's in the next release of OSF/1 source from
the OSF....


>>>Can I just change this value?

   It's pretty tricky; there's a lot of magic hacking going on that
(if I remember right) wasn't parameterized very well.


>>>Is it feasible to make a custom version of ndbm and link it
>>>with Perl? Should I just install gdbm ?

   Yes, or get the new database code from BSD 4.4; it's very nicely
done from my quick look at it a year ago...


