Hi,
I am trying to setup a cluster of alphas running DEC OSF1 V3.2, with one
of the alphas as a NIS master server.  Ideally, I would like to prevent
users from logging the server, but still be able to change passwds, 
gecos/finger information, and shell.  On an AIX cluster, there were switches
on yppasswd that allowed an user to change shell and gecos information from
any NIS client, but on the alphas these options do not seem to be present.
(yppasswd of course can change passwords for users from any client on either
cluster.)  

Does anyone know of any way to do this under Digital Unix?  Does anyone know
of sources for yppasswd and yppasswdd with this option that compile under
DEC OSF? 

A more difficult question, it would be nice to have some way to keep people
out of the server but still have a name to uid correspondence so ls -l lists
usernames not uids.  Anyone have any suggestions for doing this --- all I can
think of is /etc/nologin, which is a bit too retrictive (I would ideally like
one or two accounts other than root to log in), or to have duplicate entries
for each user, eg "user" and "(user)" with the same UID, the latter with * as 
passwd and part of a nologin netgroup and put +@nologin at the end of 
the passwd file.  The second method sounds a bit complicated (even with 
customized adduser scripts), and I expect may cause trouble, so I will probably
live with /etc/nologin unless anyone else has better ideas.

Thanks in advance for any suggestions.

Tom Payerle
payerle@physics.umd.edu



