On Mon, 07 Oct 1996 10:06:03 -0700, Patrick <pgh@rijnhaave.nl> wrote:
>Hi,
>
>When I start a script, normally a KSH is executed...
>But how do I prevent this... 
>
>why?

	It is Unix tradition that a shell script runs in a sub-shell.
	You can control what kind of shell interprets the script by
	placing a

		#! /bin/sh

	(for instance) at the left margin of the first line of the file.

>I want to start a script with some environment settings in there.
>But when I start this script, it load these setting in the
>second ksh but not in current ksh...

	Unlike DOS, each sub-shell gets a local copy of the parent's
	exported environment and may manipulate it locally;  Since a
	regular executable program has the same thing happen, and it
	is dangerous to propagate a change back to the parent process,
	this environment is _not_ returned.

	There is some logic in this.

	A dirty trick I use is to generate a file of environment variables
	and "source" it in a "current" shell using the following:

		.	./setvars

	This is how the .profile and .kshrc files are sucked in...


	UNIX *is* User-Friendly;  It's just picky about who it's friends are.

