In article <32530C5E.631F@sieweb.com>, aaron smith <aaron@sieweb.com> writes:
|>I have DEC alpha system running DEC UNIX 3.2c. I have configured it
|>for anonymous FTP which locks the user in the ftp directory structure
|>I have defined. Ninety percent of the users accounts are locked and
|>have no direct access to the filesystem. I have some users which need
|>direct access to the system via telnet and ftp. Since they are not
|>administrative users I have implemented the restricted shell
|>(/usr/bin/Rsh). I have noticed that when the user accesses the system
|>via telnet, the user is truely restricted to their home directory but
|>when the system is accessed by ftp, the user has the ability to go
|>outside his home directory. How can I prevent this from an FTP stand
|>point? I realize that I can set the permissions of various directories
|>and files so that they are not world read/writeable and modify groups,
|>but this is not practical especially since I do know the correct
|>permissions for every single file in the unix universe. So how should
|>I approach this issue? Please email your comments to
|>aaron.smith@ssc.sniemens.com as I have upgraded to sendmail8.7.6 and
|>some things are not working correctly.
|>
|>Major Thanks
|>Aaron
|>

I don't know how to do this in the Unix environment. In the VMS environment,
we found that our clients, who had Restricted and Captive accounts, could FTP
into our machine and see anything with world access, despite the Restricted/
Captive settings on their accounts. 

To get around it, we implemented Anonymous FTP, which allows someone to FTP
in using the account ANONYMOUS, and restricts them to the ANONYMOUS directory.
Then we put an Access Control List on the executable for incoming FTP
(ucx$ftpc.exe), and only gave that access to the ANONYMOUS account.

Anybody with SYSPRV or BYPASS priv can get around this, but it *did* resolve
the problem with our non-privileged users. I don't know what the equivalent
solution is on the Unix side, but I hope this helps...

